From: nuno407 <11352538+nuno407@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:20:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/45] regulator: tps65910: don't clear external sleep control
 on restart

On the Microsoft Surface RT, clearing the external sleep control of the
rails in tps65910_shutdown() hangs the SoC (vdd_cpu is under EN1 control),
so every reboot hung until the PMIC watchdog power-cycled it (~100 s).
Found by logging device shutdown with the eMMC kept alive: the last device
was tps65910-pmic. A restart goes through the PMIC (DEV_OFF_RST), which
resets those registers anyway, so skip it on restart.
---
 drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c
index 3a3027e0b..f31104d07 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c
@@ -1226,6 +1226,15 @@ static void tps65910_shutdown(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	struct tps65910_reg *pmic = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 	int i;
 
+	/*
+	 * A restart goes through the PMIC itself (DEV_OFF_RST), which resets
+	 * these registers anyway. On the Microsoft Surface RT, whose vdd_cpu
+	 * is under EN1 external control, clearing the external control here
+	 * hangs the SoC before the restart handler runs.
+	 */
+	if (system_state == SYSTEM_RESTART)
+		return;
+
 	/*
 	 * Before bootloader jumps to kernel, it makes sure that required
 	 * external control signals are in desired state so that given rails
